FOID LICENSE
Version 1.0, August 2026

Copyright (c) 2026 FOID Linux Contributors

Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy
of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights
to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell
copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is
furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:

1. The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in
   all copies or substantial portions of the Software.

2. THE FOID CLAUSE: If you fork this project, do not call it "FOID" or use
   the FOID logo/name for your fork. Make your own name. Be independent.

3. THE NO-BLOAT CLAUSE: This Software may not be used in any project,
   distribution, or system that depends on systemd as its init system.

   For example, if your OS boots with `systemctl`, `journalctl`, or any binary
   from the systemd project residing in /lib/systemd/, you are in violation.
   If your init is /sbin/init and it is a symlink to systemd, you are in
   violation. If your boot process takes longer than 5 seconds because something
   is "waiting for network", you are in violation.

   RAM-INCOMPATIBLE SOFTWARE includes, but is not limited to: GNOME (all versions
   past 2.x), KDE Plasma, Electron-based applications (Discord, Spotify, VS Code,
   Slack, any "app" that ships a 200MB tarball containing an entire Chromium),
   Docker (unless running rootless with a single Alpine container), Snap,
   Flatpak (unless the runtime is smaller than 50MB), and any process whose
   RSS exceeds 512MB while idle. If your browser uses more RAM than the Linux
   kernel, you are in violation. If you run `htop` and the first screen is
   already full, you are in violation.

   If you need an init system, use something that respects your RAM.

4. THE FEDORA CLAUSE: Do not mention "Fedora" in connection with this
   Software. We do not talk about Fedora. We do not think about Fedora.
   This clause exists because we said so.

5. THE VOID CLAUSE: If you are migrating from Void Linux, you may skip
   clauses 3 and 4 for a grace period of 30 days. We understand. Take your time.

6. THE ARCH CLAUSE: Users of Arch Linux are permitted to mention they use
   Arch, but only once per conversation. Further mentions constitute spam and
   void this license. We know. You told us.

7. THE NEOVIM CLAUSE: If you use Neovim, you are contractually obligated to
   tell everyone about your init.lua at least once. If you use Emacs, you must
   defend your choice in a 500-word essay. If you use nano, you are valid and
   protected under this license.

8. THE WAYLAND CLAUSE: If this Software is running under X11 in the year 2026
   or later, the user must provide a written apology to their GPU. Exceptions
   are made for users of dwm, because they have suffered enough.

9. THE COMPILER CLAUSE: This Software may be compiled with GCC, Clang, or
   TCC. Compilation with proprietary compilers is permitted but frowned upon.
   If you manage to compile this with a compiler you wrote yourself in a
   weekend, you automatically become a maintainer.

10. THE TERMINAL CLAUSE: The user agrees that `ls` is a valid file manager,
    `curl | sh` is a valid package manager, and `cat` is a valid pager. Anyone
    who says otherwise may be ignored.

11. THE DOTFILES CLAUSE: Every user of this Software must maintain a public
    GitHub repository named dotfiles containing at least one symlink script
    that breaks on any machine other than their own. Failure to maintain such
    a repository shall result in immediate revocation of ricing privileges.

12. THE RICING CLAUSE: The user acknowledges that spending 6 hours configuring
    a status bar is a valid use of time. However, if the resulting rice uses
    more than 16MB of RAM, the user must publicly admit that their aesthetic
    choices have consequences.

13. THE DISTROHOPPING CLAUSE: If the user has installed more than 3 Linux
    distributions in the past calendar month, they are required to pick one
    and delete the others. Virtual machines count. Live USBs count. That one
    partition you forgot about counts. Pick one. We are waiting.

14. THE GENTOO CLAUSE: Gentoo users are exempt from all performance-related
    clauses on the grounds that they have already punished themselves enough.
    However, they must agree that CFLAGS="-O3 -march=native" does not actually
    make Firefox faster, and they must say this out loud at least once.

15. THE NIX CLAUSE: NixOS users may use this Software, but they must explain
    how flakes work to at least one confused Ubuntu user before the end of the
    fiscal year. If they cannot explain it, they must admit that they too are
    confused, which is legally binding.

16. THE DOCKER CLAUSE: Running this Software inside a Docker container is
    permitted only if the Dockerfile is smaller than 10 lines and does not use
    Alpine (because that would be too easy). If your Docker image has 47 layers,
    you must write a 3-page apology to the inode table.

17. THE README CLAUSE: Any project derived from this Software must include a
    README.md that starts with the words "FOID-inspired" and contains at least
    one ASCII art logo. The logo must be hand-drawn; generators are prohibited
    and punishable by forced use of Comic Sans.

18. THE ISSUE CLAUSE: Before opening an issue, the user must run `rm -rf /`
    in a VM to ensure the problem is reproducible. If the user is unwilling
    to do this, they may instead provide a minimal working example, but we
    prefer the VM method because it builds character.

19. THE PR CLAUSE: All pull requests must be accompanied by a haiku describing
    the change. The haiku must follow the 5-7-5 syllable structure. Pull
    requests that fix typos are exempt, but only if the typo was genuinely
    funny.

20. THE FORMATTING CLAUSE: This Software may be indented with tabs or spaces,
    but the user must pick one and defend their choice in a flame war at
    least once in their lifetime. Users who indent with 8 spaces are legally
    classified as performance artists.

21. THE LANGUAGE CLAUSE: This Software may be rewritten in any programming
    language, provided the rewrite is justified by a 200-word manifesto posted
    to Hacker News. Rewrites in Rust require only 50 words but must include
    the phrase "zero-cost abstractions" at least twice.

22. THE FONT CLAUSE: The user agrees that bitmap fonts are underrated, that
    JetBrains Mono is acceptable, and that using a font with ligatures in a
    terminal is a personal choice that we respect but do not understand.
    Users of Comic Sans are reported to the authorities.

23. THE WALLPAPER CLAUSE: The user's desktop wallpaper must not contain a
    photograph of a mountain, a forest, or an anime character unless the user
    can prove they have visited that mountain, planted a tree in that forest,
    or legally changed their name to match that character.

24. THE SLEEP CLAUSE: The user acknowledges that compiling a Linux kernel at
    3:47 AM is a valid lifestyle choice. However, if the user falls asleep
    during compilation and the laptop overheats, the user must buy the
    contributors a new laptop. This is non-negotiable.

25. THE MOUSE CLAUSE: Use of a computer mouse is permitted but discouraged.
    If the user must use a mouse, they are required to feel a mild sense of
    shame. Trackball users are exempt because they have already made their
    choice and we respect commitment.

26. THE KEYBOARD CLAUSE: The user must be able to touch-type at least 40 WPM
    or admit that they are "still learning" for the 7th consecutive year.
    Users of QWERTY, Dvorak, Colemak, and Workman are all valid. Users of
    custom firmware on their mechanical keyboard must disclose their switch
    type within 30 seconds of being asked.

27. THE SWAP CLAUSE: The user agrees that swap partitions are a conspiracy
    designed to slow down SSDs. If the user has more than 8GB of RAM and still
    uses a swap file, they must explain why to a rubber duck for no less than
    15 minutes.

28. THE LOG CLAUSE: The user shall not fill /var/log with 40GB of journal
    entries. If the user requires logging, they may use `echo` redirected to
    a file. If that file exceeds 1MB, the user must rotate it manually while
    contemplating the impermanence of all things.

29. THE UPTIME CLAUSE: If the user's uptime exceeds 365 days, they are
    required to reboot immediately and then lie about how long it was.
    Alternatively, they may apply security patches like a responsible adult.

30. THE MAN PAGE CLAUSE: The user agrees to read at least one man page per
    week. If the user Googles "how to [basic command]" instead of running
    `man [basic command]`, they must write the missing man page themselves
    and submit it upstream.

31. THE ALIAS CLAUSE: The user may not alias `rm` to `rm -i`. If they do,
    they must also alias `cd` to `echo "you are already home"` and `ls` to
    `echo "just use find"`. Fair is fair.

32. THE SUDO CLAUSE: The user must type their password correctly on the first
    try at least 60% of the time. If their accuracy falls below this threshold,
    they are required to switch to `doas` and never look back.

33. THE BACKUP CLAUSE: The user acknowledges that they have no backups. They
    have always had no backups. They will continue to have no backups until
    the day after a catastrophic failure, at which point they will consider
    making backups and then not do it.

34. THE WIFI CLAUSE: If the user's Wi-Fi stops working after a kernel update,
    they are prohibited from blaming the distro. They must instead write a
    polite email to the firmware maintainer and then switch to Ethernet forever.

35. THE BLUETOOTH CLAUSE: Bluetooth is permitted but only for headphones.
    If the user attempts to use Bluetooth for file transfer, they must first
    explain why they are not using `rsync` over SSH like a civilized person.

36. THE BATTERY CLAUSE: If the user's laptop battery health drops below 70%,
    they are required to acknowledge that they have been leaving it plugged in
    at 100% for three years and that this was, in retrospect, a poor decision.

37. THE MONITOR CLAUSE: The user agrees that one monitor is enough. Two
    monitors are acceptable. Three monitors suggest a problem. Four monitors
    require a written explanation to the contributors. Ultrawide users are
    valid but suspicious.

38. THE NOTIFICATION CLAUSE: The user shall not enable desktop notifications
    for Discord, Slack, or any application that exists solely to interrupt
    workflow. If the user requires notifications, they may use `dunst` with
    a 10-second timeout and a sense of regret.

39. THE SCREENSHOT CLAUSE: Any screenshot of this Software shared to r/unixporn
    must include at least one terminal window showing `neofetch` or `fastfetch`
    output. Screenshots showing only a wallpaper will be met with mild
    disappointment.

40. THE PACKAGE COUNT CLAUSE: The user agrees that having fewer than 500
    installed packages is a valid flex. Having more than 2000 packages is not
    a flex; it is a cry for help. The user must audit their dependencies and
    delete at least one package they do not recognize.

41. THE KERNEL CLAUSE: The user may compile their own kernel, but they must
    admit that they only changed three options and that two of them were
    mistakes. If the user claims they need a custom kernel for "performance",
    they must provide benchmark data or remain silent.

42. THE MODULE CLAUSE: If the user runs `lsmod` and sees more than 50 modules,
    they are required to research what each one does. If they cannot explain
    `nf_conntrack`, they must blacklist it and deal with the consequences.

43. THE FIREWALL CLAUSE: The user agrees that `iptables -F` is not a valid
    troubleshooting step. If the user runs this command, they must immediately
    disconnect from the internet and rethink their life choices.

44. THE PASSWORD CLAUSE: The user's passwords must be at least 16 characters,
    contain no dictionary words, and be stored in a password manager. If the
    user writes passwords in a text file named `passwords.txt`, this license
    self-destructs.

45. THE SSH CLAUSE: The user must use key-based authentication. Password
    authentication over SSH is permitted only if the password is "correcthorse
    batterystaple" and the user is fully aware of the xkcd reference.

46. THE PORT CLAUSE: The user agrees that port 22 is boring. If they must
    change the SSH port, they should pick something memorable like 2222 or
    something chaotic like 49152. Port 1337 is acceptable but cliché.

47. THE DNS CLAUSE: The user must use a DNS server they can name. If they
    are using their ISP's DNS because "it just works", they must look up what
    DNS is and then switch to 9.9.9.9 or 1.1.1.1 out of spite.

48. THE VPN CLAUSE: The user may use a VPN but must be able to explain what
    a VPN actually does. If they say "it makes me anonymous", they must watch
    a 20-minute YouTube video about how the internet works and then try again.

49. THE TOR CLAUSE: Tor users are respected but must admit that they are
    mostly using it to read Wikipedia articles that are blocked in their
    country. This is valid and protected.

50. THE IRC CLAUSE: The user agrees that IRC is not dead. If they claim it
    is dead, they must join a channel, stay for 48 hours, and then apologize
    to the people who have been there since 1998.

51. THE MATRIX CLAUSE: Matrix users are permitted but must explain why they
    need end-to-end encryption for a channel where the only messages are
    "ping" and "pong".

52. THE EMAIL CLAUSE: The user must check their email at least once per week.
    If they have more than 1000 unread emails, they must declare email
    bankruptcy and start over with a new address.

53. THE CALENDAR CLAUSE: The user agrees that `cal` is a valid calendar
    application. If they require a GUI calendar, they must admit that they
    only use it to count down to the next kernel release.

54. THE MUSIC CLAUSE: The user may listen to music while coding, but they
    must not listen to lo-fi hip hop radio beats to relax/study to. That
    channel is exhausted. Try dark ambient or nothing.

55. THE COFFEE CLAUSE: The user agrees that coffee is a performance-enhancing
    drug and that their dependency is neither a problem nor a solution. Tea
    drinkers are valid but must stop pretending it is the same thing.

56. THE ENERGY DRINK CLAUSE: Energy drinks are permitted only during kernel
    panics and compiler errors. If the user drinks an energy drink while
    reading documentation, they must question their time management skills.

57. THE CHAIR CLAUSE: The user acknowledges that their chair is terrible
    and that they should buy a better one. They will not buy a better one.
    This is understood.

58. THE DESK CLAUSE: The user's desk must contain at least one item that
    serves no purpose. If everything on the desk is functional, the user is
    suspected of being an alien and must provide proof of humanity.

59. THE CABLE CLAUSE: The user agrees that cable management is a myth. If
    they claim to have good cable management, they must post a photo of the
    back of their desk. We all know what is back there. It is okay.

60. THE RGB CLAUSE: RGB lighting is permitted but must be set to a single
    color. Rainbow puke mode is prohibited unless the user is under 16 years
    of age. If the user is over 30 and still uses rainbow mode, they must
    explain their emotional state in writing.

61. THE FAN CLAUSE: The user agrees that fan noise is white noise and that
    a silent PC is suspicious. If the user's PC is completely silent, they
    must check if it is still on.

62. THE THERMAL CLAUSE: If the user's CPU exceeds 85°C, they are required to
    clean their heatsink. They will not clean their heatsink. They will
    instead install a new fan and hope for the best.

63. THE GPU CLAUSE: NVIDIA users are permitted but must acknowledge that
    they are in a complicated relationship. AMD users must stop talking about
    how open the drivers are. Intel GPU users are supported and protected.

64. THE DRIVER CLAUSE: If the user installs a proprietary driver, they must
    whisper "I'm sorry, Richard" every time they boot. If they do not know
    who Richard is, they must look it up and then whisper anyway.

65. THE FIRMWARE CLAUSE: The user agrees that firmware blobs are a necessary
    evil. They do not have to like them. They just have to accept them and
    move on.

66. THE BIOS CLAUSE: The user must enter their BIOS/UEFI settings at least
    once per year. If they have never entered the BIOS, they must do so now
    and then immediately leave because it is scary in there.

67. THE BOOTLOADER CLAUSE: The user may use GRUB, systemd-boot (wait, no,
    clause 3), Limine, or rEFInd. If they chainload more than three
    bootloaders, they must explain why in a 12-step program.

68. THE PARTITION CLAUSE: The user agrees that they have at least one
    partition they forgot about. It is probably 8GB and labeled "old stuff".
    They will never delete it. This is fine.

69. THE FILESYSTEM CLAUSE: The user may use ext4, xfs, or btrfs. If they
    use btrfs, they must admit that they only use it for snapshots and that
    they have never actually restored from one. ZFS users are respected but
    must stop talking about ARC.

70. THE MOUNT CLAUSE: The user agrees that `mount` without arguments is a
    valid way to spend 20 minutes reading about their own system. If they
    use `findmnt` instead, they are advanced and slightly intimidating.

71. THE SYMLINK CLAUSE: The user must have created at least one symlink
    that points nowhere. If all their symlinks are valid, they are either
    lying or have not lived enough.

72. THE PERMISSION CLAUSE: The user agrees that `chmod 777` is never the
    answer. If they run it anyway, they must wear a sign that says "I gave
    up" for the remainder of the day.

73. THE OWNERSHIP CLAUSE: If the user runs `chown -R root:root /` by
    accident, they are required to reinstall and tell no one. This is a
    binding secret.

74. THE PIPE CLAUSE: The user agrees that `|` is the most powerful character
    in computing. If they do not understand pipes, they must read the
    manual and then write a sonnet about data flow.

75. THE REGEX CLAUSE: The user may use regular expressions, but they must
    admit that they had to look up the syntax. If they claim to know regex
    by heart, they must write a parser for HTML using only regex and then
    apologize to the Computer Science community.

76. THE SED CLAUSE: The user agrees that `sed` is a valid text editor for
    one-line changes. If they write a 400-character sed command, they must
    rewrite it in Python and then question their life choices.

77. THE AWK CLAUSE: AWK users are respected elders. If the user does not
    know AWK, they are not required to learn it, but they must nod politely
    when someone mentions it.

78. THE PERL CLAUSE: Perl is not mentioned in this license. If you are
    reading this, you are not using Perl. Good.

79. THE PYTHON CLAUSE: Python is permitted for scripts under 100 lines.
    If the script exceeds 100 lines, the user must rewrite it in Go, Rust,
    or C, or admit that they enjoy dynamic typing more than they should.

80. THE RUBY CLAUSE: Ruby users are valid but must explain what they are
    doing in 2026. If the answer is "Jekyll", they are forgiven. If the
    answer is "Rails", they must defend their stack in a public forum.

81. THE JAVA CLAUSE: Java is not prohibited, but the user must acknowledge
    that they are running a JVM to run a build tool to compile a program
    that prints "Hello World" and that this is, technically, a choice.

82. THE JAVASCRIPT CLAUSE: JavaScript is permitted only in the browser.
    If the user runs JavaScript on the server, they must explain why they
    need 400MB of node_modules to serve a static page.

83. THE TYPESCRIPT CLAUSE: TypeScript users must admit that the types are
    mostly `any` and that the `tsconfig.json` was copied from Stack Overflow.

84. THE CSS CLAUSE: The user agrees that CSS is a puzzle and that centering
    a div is the final boss. If they claim CSS is easy, they must center a
    div vertically and horizontally without using flexbox or grid.

85. THE HTML CLAUSE: The user agrees that HTML is a programming language
    if they say it is. We will not argue. Life is too short.

86. THE SQL CLAUSE: The user must write at least one SQL query per year
    by hand. If they only use ORMs, they must look at the generated SQL
    and feel a vague sense of unease.

87. THE JSON CLAUSE: The user agrees that JSON is fine. XML users must
    explain why they need closing tags for everything. YAML users must
    admit that they have been burned by indentation at least once.

88. THE TOML CLAUSE: TOML users are valid but must stop telling everyone
    how readable it is. We have heard. It is readable. Thank you.

89. THE GIT CLAUSE: The user agrees that `git push --force` is a war crime.
    If they use it, they must notify their team in advance and then accept
    that they are the villain now.

90. THE COMMIT CLAUSE: Commit messages must be meaningful. "fix stuff" is
    not meaningful. "asdf" is not meaningful. If the user cannot describe
    the change in 50 characters, they must reconsider the change.

91. THE BRANCH CLAUSE: The user may have as many local branches as they
    want, but they must delete at least one per month. If they have 47
    branches named "fix-thing-final-FOR-REAL-2", they must face judgment.

92. THE MERGE CLAUSE: Merge commits are valid. Rebase is also valid. The
    user must pick one and then argue about it online at least once. If they
    do not care, they must pretend to care for 5 minutes.

93. THE STASH CLAUSE: The user agrees that `git stash` is where code goes
    to die. If they have stashes older than 6 months, they must delete them
    and accept that they will never remember what "wip" meant.

94. THE SUBMODULE CLAUSE: Git submodules are prohibited. If the user thinks
    they need submodules, they need monorepo tooling. If they think they
    need monorepo tooling, they need to simplify their project.

95. THE TODO CLAUSE: The user must have at least one `TODO` comment in
    their codebase that is over 2 years old. If all TODOs are resolved,
    they are either lying or have achieved enlightenment.

96. THE COMMENT CLAUSE: Comments are permitted but must not explain what the
    code does. They must explain why the code exists. If the comment says
    "increment i", the user must delete it and feel shame.

97. THE DEBUG CLAUSE: The user agrees that `printf` debugging is a valid
    strategy. If they use a debugger, they must admit that they only use
    10% of its features and that the other 90% are witchcraft.

98. THE STACK TRACE CLAUSE: When the user encounters a stack trace, they
    must read it. All of it. Not just the first line. The answer is in
    line 47 and they know it.

99. THE RTFM CLAUSE: The user agrees that the answer is in the manual.
    It is always in the manual. If they refuse to read the manual, they
    must accept that their suffering is self-inflicted and that no one
    feels sorry for them.

100. If this Software saves you from systemd, you owe the contributors a beer
     (or a coffee, or a thought — we are not picky).

